NASM Requires Square Brackets For Memory References
mov ax,[bar]
a86, mov ax,var / var: dw 0 / defferent behavior

NASM doesn't support the LODS, MOVS, STOS, SCAS, CMPS, INS, or OUTS instructions
supports the forms such as LODSB, MOVSW, and SCASD / which explicitly specify the size of the components of the strings being manipulated

NASM will not keep track of what values you choose to put in your segment registers, and will never automatically generate a segment override prefix

NASM Doesn't Support Memory Models
RETN or RETF; NASM accepts RET itself as an alternate form for RETN
NASM also does not have any directives to support different 16-bit memory models

Floating-Point Differences
where MASM would call them ST(0), ST(1) and so on, and a86 would call them simply 0, 1 and so on, NASM chooses to call them st0, st1

